RM  29382829
RT  Type IX secretion system PorM and gliding machinery GldM form arches
RT  spanning the periplasmic space.
RA  Leone P, Roche J, Vincent MS, Tran QH, Desmyter A, Cascales E,
RA  Kellenberger C, Cambillau C, Roussel A;
RL  Nat Commun. 2018;9(1):429.
CC  The type IX secretion system (T9SS), also known as the Por secretion
CC  system (PorSS), is a protein export apparatus restricted to the
CC  Fibrobacteres-Chlorobi-Bacteroidetes (FCB) superphylum and most
CC  prominently characterised in the Bacteroidota. The system was first
CC  defined in the periodontal pathogen Porphyromonas gingivalis, where
CC  it secretes the gingipain virulence proteases, and in the gliding
CC  bacterium Flavobacterium johnsoniae, where it exports cell-surface
CC  motility adhesins (SprB, RemA) and biopolymer-degrading enzymes.
CC  T9SS substrates carry an N-terminal Sec signal peptide that targets
CC  them to the periplasm, plus a conserved C-terminal domain (CTD) of
CC  either type A (TIGR04183) or type B (TIGR04131; Pfam PF13585) that
CC  serves as the T9SS-specific outer-membrane translocation signal.
CC  Translocation is performed by the SprA/Sov outer-membrane beta-barrel
CC  translocon, energised via the proton-motive force harvested by the
CC  PorL/GldL-PorM/GldM motor at the inner membrane and transmitted
CC  through the periplasmic PorK/GldK-PorN/GldN ring complex (with
CC  PorW/SprE bridging the ring to the translocon). At the cell surface,
CC  an attachment complex of PorU (sortase / C-terminal signal
CC  peptidase), PorV (shuttle), PorQ and PorZ cleaves the CTD and, in
CC  P. gingivalis, conjugates the cargo to anionic lipopolysaccharide
CC  (A-LPS). Most species additionally encode PorT/SprT and PorP/SprF.
